\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Temporal dynamics and choice-dependent updating of resource-state representations.} \textbf  {(A) Temporal dynamics of resource-state representations.} Columns show pooled population data from ACC, DLPFC and OFC. Each entry reports the cross-validated $R^2$ obtained using a TDR-derived resource-state axis trained at one time bin and tested at another. The four blocks comprise decoding within the offer-onset epoch (upper left; 14 bins, $-200$ to $500$~ms relative to offer onset), within the wallet-update epoch (lower right; 10 bins, $-200$ to $300$~ms relative to wallet update), and across the two epochs (upper right and lower left); bin width was 50~ms. Horizontal and vertical white dashed lines mark offer onset and wallet update. Black entries did not significantly exceed the shuffle control (one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test, $p>0.05$). Decoding was concentrated near the temporal diagonal during the offer-onset epoch but generalized broadly across the wallet-update epoch. \textbf  {(B) Population trajectories during wallet update.} Neural population activity from $-100$ to $200$~ms relative to wallet update was projected onto TDR-derived choice and resource-state axes. The choice axis was oriented from spending (left) to earning (right), and the resource-state axis from lower (bottom) to higher (top) token holdings. Red and green trajectories denote earning and spending trials, respectively; darker shades denote lower resource states. Circles and triangles mark the neural states at $-100$~ms for earning and spending trials, respectively, from which the trajectories evolve over time. \textbf  {(C) Quantification of directional resource-state updating.} For each bootstrap iteration, the resource-state-axis projection was regressed against time separately for earning and spending trials. The resulting temporal coefficient, $\beta _{\mathrm  {update}}$, is positive for movement toward a higher resource state and negative for movement toward a lower resource state. Histograms show the distributions of $\beta _{\mathrm  {update}}$ across bootstrap iterations for earning (red) and spending (green); the two distributions are mirrored horizontally for visualization, whereas update direction is indicated by the sign of $\beta _{\mathrm  {update}}$ on the vertical axis. Triangles mark median coefficients; filled and open triangles indicate significant and non-significant shifts, respectively. Coefficients were tested against zero using one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (right-tailed for earning; left-tailed for spending). Choice-dependent resource-state updating required both a significantly positive earning coefficient and a significantly negative spending coefficient; ACC and OFC, but not DLPFC, satisfied this bidirectional criterion.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:resource_state_dynamics}{{3}{6}{\textbf {Temporal dynamics and choice-dependent updating of resource-state representations.} \textbf {(A) Temporal dynamics of resource-state representations.} Columns show pooled population data from ACC, DLPFC and OFC. Each entry reports the cross-validated $R^2$ obtained using a TDR-derived resource-state axis trained at one time bin and tested at another. The four blocks comprise decoding within the offer-onset epoch (upper left; 14 bins, $-200$ to $500$~ms relative to offer onset), within the wallet-update epoch (lower right; 10 bins, $-200$ to $300$~ms relative to wallet update), and across the two epochs (upper right and lower left); bin width was 50~ms. Horizontal and vertical white dashed lines mark offer onset and wallet update. Black entries did not significantly exceed the shuffle control (one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test, $p>0.05$). Decoding was concentrated near the temporal diagonal during the offer-onset epoch but generalized broadly across the wallet-update epoch. \textbf {(B) Population trajectories during wallet update.} Neural population activity from $-100$ to $200$~ms relative to wallet update was projected onto TDR-derived choice and resource-state axes. The choice axis was oriented from spending (left) to earning (right), and the resource-state axis from lower (bottom) to higher (top) token holdings. Red and green trajectories denote earning and spending trials, respectively; darker shades denote lower resource states. Circles and triangles mark the neural states at $-100$~ms for earning and spending trials, respectively, from which the trajectories evolve over time. \textbf {(C) Quantification of directional resource-state updating.} For each bootstrap iteration, the resource-state-axis projection was regressed against time separately for earning and spending trials. The resulting temporal coefficient, $\beta _{\mathrm {update}}$, is positive for movement toward a higher resource state and negative for movement toward a lower resource state. Histograms show the distributions of $\beta _{\mathrm {update}}$ across bootstrap iterations for earning (red) and spending (green); the two distributions are mirrored horizontally for visualization, whereas update direction is indicated by the sign of $\beta _{\mathrm {update}}$ on the vertical axis. Triangles mark median coefficients; filled and open triangles indicate significant and non-significant shifts, respectively. Coefficients were tested against zero using one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (right-tailed for earning; left-tailed for spending). Choice-dependent resource-state updating required both a significantly positive earning coefficient and a significantly negative spending coefficient; ACC and OFC, but not DLPFC, satisfied this bidirectional criterion}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces  \textbf  {Resource-state modulation of prefrontal offer-value representations.} \textbf  {(A--B) Behavioral-model predictions.} Model-derived earning value ($V_E$; A) and spending value ($V_S$; B) are plotted across five token-holding groups ([1], [2--3], [4--7], [8--15] and [16--24] tokens). In A, the three red curves correspond to earning rates of 4, 5 and 10 tokens~s$^{-1}$; darker red denotes a higher earning rate. In B, the three green curves correspond to the three non-zero spending reward magnitudes; darker green denotes a larger reward. The zero-reward spending condition was omitted because monkeys rarely chose this option, yielding insufficient resource-state-update observations for stable condition averages. As resource state increased, the model predicted a narrowing of the range separating earning values ($V_E$ range, red) but a comparatively preserved range separating spending values ($V_S$ range, green). \textbf  {(C) Incremental variance explained by resource-state-modulated earning value.} Solid lines show the across-unit mean increase in explained variance when the resource-state-dependent earning value $V_E$ was added to the baseline model ($M_1$) to form $M_2$, aligned to resource-state update; shading denotes $\pm $SEM. Red, green and blue denote ACC, DLPFC and OFC, respectively; dashed lines show the corresponding value-label-shuffled controls. The vertical dashed line marks resource-state update. Colored triangles denote time bins in which observed $\Delta R^2_{\mathrm  {adj}}$ differed from the shuffled control ($p<0.05$, two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test). Corresponding individual-animal results are shown in Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:supp_offer_value_single_unit}D. \textbf  {(D) Population value spaces across resource states.} TDR identified a two-dimensional value space with an earning-value axis ($V_E$, horizontal) and a spending-value axis ($V_S$, vertical). Columns show ACC, DLPFC and OFC; rows show low (1 token), middle (2--5 tokens) and high (6--24 tokens) resource states. Each point is the condition-averaged population state for one earning--spending offer combination during the $-200$ to $300$~ms window around resource-state update. The nine points correspond to the $3\times 3$ subset of the behavioral condition matrix (Fig.~\ref {fig:behavior}B) obtained by crossing the three earning rates with the three non-zero spending rewards; the zero-reward spending column was excluded for the sampling reason noted above. Point hues follow the offer-condition color map in Fig.~\ref {fig:behavior}B, from conditions relatively favoring spending (green) to those relatively favoring earning (brown). Lighter shades denote higher resource states. Dotted lines connect neighboring conditions in the $3\times 3$ offer matrix to make its geometry visible; they do not represent neural trajectories or temporal order. Red and green arrows illustrate the ranges occupied along the $V_E$ and $V_S$ axes, respectively. \textbf  {(E) Aspect ratio of the population value space.} For ACC, DLPFC and OFC, the spending-axis range was divided by the earning-axis range within each resource-state group. Points and error bars show the mean $\pm $SD across bootstrap resamples; the red dotted line marks an aspect ratio of 1. Component earning- and spending-axis ranges, shuffle controls and individual-animal results are shown in Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:supp_offer_value_geometry}.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:value_modulation}{{4}{7}{\textbf {Resource-state modulation of prefrontal offer-value representations.} \textbf {(A--B) Behavioral-model predictions.} Model-derived earning value ($V_E$; A) and spending value ($V_S$; B) are plotted across five token-holding groups ([1], [2--3], [4--7], [8--15] and [16--24] tokens). In A, the three red curves correspond to earning rates of 4, 5 and 10 tokens~s$^{-1}$; darker red denotes a higher earning rate. In B, the three green curves correspond to the three non-zero spending reward magnitudes; darker green denotes a larger reward. The zero-reward spending condition was omitted because monkeys rarely chose this option, yielding insufficient resource-state-update observations for stable condition averages. As resource state increased, the model predicted a narrowing of the range separating earning values ($V_E$ range, red) but a comparatively preserved range separating spending values ($V_S$ range, green). \textbf {(C) Incremental variance explained by resource-state-modulated earning value.} Solid lines show the across-unit mean increase in explained variance when the resource-state-dependent earning value $V_E$ was added to the baseline model ($M_1$) to form $M_2$, aligned to resource-state update; shading denotes $\pm $SEM. Red, green and blue denote ACC, DLPFC and OFC, respectively; dashed lines show the corresponding value-label-shuffled controls. The vertical dashed line marks resource-state update. Colored triangles denote time bins in which observed $\Delta R^2_{\mathrm {adj}}$ differed from the shuffled control ($p<0.05$, two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test). Corresponding individual-animal results are shown in Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:supp_offer_value_single_unit}D. \textbf {(D) Population value spaces across resource states.} TDR identified a two-dimensional value space with an earning-value axis ($V_E$, horizontal) and a spending-value axis ($V_S$, vertical). Columns show ACC, DLPFC and OFC; rows show low (1 token), middle (2--5 tokens) and high (6--24 tokens) resource states. Each point is the condition-averaged population state for one earning--spending offer combination during the $-200$ to $300$~ms window around resource-state update. The nine points correspond to the $3\times 3$ subset of the behavioral condition matrix (Fig.~\ref {fig:behavior}B) obtained by crossing the three earning rates with the three non-zero spending rewards; the zero-reward spending column was excluded for the sampling reason noted above. Point hues follow the offer-condition color map in Fig.~\ref {fig:behavior}B, from conditions relatively favoring spending (green) to those relatively favoring earning (brown). Lighter shades denote higher resource states. Dotted lines connect neighboring conditions in the $3\times 3$ offer matrix to make its geometry visible; they do not represent neural trajectories or temporal order. Red and green arrows illustrate the ranges occupied along the $V_E$ and $V_S$ axes, respectively. \textbf {(E) Aspect ratio of the population value space.} For ACC, DLPFC and OFC, the spending-axis range was divided by the earning-axis range within each resource-state group. Points and error bars show the mean $\pm $SD across bootstrap resamples; the red dotted line marks an aspect ratio of 1. Component earning- and spending-axis ranges, shuffle controls and individual-animal results are shown in Supplementary Fig.~\ref {fig:supp_offer_value_geometry}}{figure.4}{}}
